                    },
                    "message": "```suggestion\n    - name: BoostClockFrequency\n```\n\nI would suggest using the base unit (Hertz)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/Accelerator.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 8,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/Accelerator.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 8,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't understand why two of Alexander's feedback was \"Acknowledged\" with no change and no rationale.  Please follow up with rationale.\n\n(I'm not saying I agree with one direction or the other at this time.)"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/Accelerator.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 8,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "I meant to push the change but got side tracked. I'll push a change soon; thanks for the reminder. I suppose the base unit is more usable across implementations. I'll look at existing examples."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/Accelerator.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 8,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "Thinking more about this, Mhz is a better option I think as it is a more realiatic unit (or easily converted to Ghz) than the base unit. This API is not too generic. We also have a CPU clock in a different interface which is also Mhz"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/Accelerator.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 8,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "This is `lscpu` from my 9elements-provided work laptop (some lines removed to keep it short):\n\n```\n[alex@thonkpadT14 ~]$ lscpu\nArchitecture:                x86_64\n  CPU op-mode(s):            32-bit, 64-bit\n  Address sizes:             48 bits physical, 48 bits virtual\n  Byte Order:                Little Endian\nCPU(s):                      16\n  On-line CPU(s) list:       0-15\nVendor ID:                   AuthenticAMD\n  Model name:                AMD Ryzen 7 PRO 6850U with Radeon Graphics\n    CPU family:              25\n    Model:                   68\n    Thread(s) per core:      2\n    Core(s) per socket:      8\n    Socket(s):               1\n    Stepping:                1\n    Frequency boost:         enabled\n    CPU(s) scaling MHz:      32%\n    CPU max MHz:             4769.8521\n    CPU min MHz:             402.2770\n...\n```\n\nAs you can see the CPU max clock frequency is not a multiple of 1 MHz.\n\nSo the value would be `4769852100 Hz` in my case.\n\n> Mhz is a better option I think as it is a more realiatic unit (or easily converted to Ghz) than the base unit.\n\n\nIf you want an integer value, using the base unit is more realistic since it's 'closer' to the real value.\n\n> or easily converted to Ghz\n\nHow is it more difficult, we just divide by `10^9` instead of `10^3`\n\n\nIf you just want a *display number* that must not match the actual max frequency then let's get it documented in the interface description that it is just for display and does not match the actual max frequency.\n\n\n> We also have a CPU clock in a different interface which is also Mhz\n\nDoes that then also need changing or is it also just for display purpose?\nMaybe i have some misunderstanding here, or got a wrong idea? Please let me know \ud83d\ude0a"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/Accelerator.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 8,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "I think what you suggest is reasonable; will change to the base unit."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/Accelerator.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Harshit Aghera",
                        "email": "haghera@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "harshit-aghera"
                    },
                    "message": "The maxint here means maximum value of uint64?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/Accelerator.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "No one should have to answer for you what can be determined from the documentation on the YAML format.\n\n```\ndocs/yaml/interface.md\n108:- `maxint` - The maximum value the integer type can hold.\n```"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/Accelerator.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Harshit Aghera",
                        "email": "haghera@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "harshit-aghera"
                    },
                    "message": "Why maximum value of int is chosen as default value for type uint64? Wouldn't maximum value of uint64 or even 0 be a preferred alternative?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/Accelerator.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "Sdbusplus maps maxint to std::numeric_limits with the correct cpp type. I prefer maxint over 0 because the former is a documented special value."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/Accelerator.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Deepak Kodihalli",
                        "email": "deepak.kodihalli.83@gmail.com",
                        "username": "dkodihal"
                    },
                    "message": "Resolving this."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Inventory/Item/Accelerator.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
